{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "# ML-09 — Validation and Research Claim Audit\n\n[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/AbdulWasay65/flyrank-ml-internship/blob/main/work/notebooks/w06_validation_audit.ipynb?flush_cache=true)\n\nThis notebook audits the Week-5 Logistic Regression model using FlyRank's leakage and validation guidance. The goal is honest, directional decision-support — not causal proof or a guarantee of future ranking improvement."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 1. Two paper findings + my methodology questions\n\n### Finding 1 — The Content Results Curve\n\nFlyRank reports that health score rises through the 61–90 day content-age bucket and then generally declines, with the 271–365 day bucket lower than the 61–90 day bucket. The paper describes this as an observed content life-cycle pattern.\n\n**My methodology question:** The outcome here is a composite Health Score rather than a single downstream business outcome. How exactly is the label/outcome constructed for each page and age bucket, and could components of the score (impressions, position, CTR, scroll depth) also be related to the way the age groups are selected or compared? For the claim that results weaken after about nine months, does the validation design separate age effects from other differences between older and newer pages, such as site mix, content type, or historical visibility?\n\nI would treat the result as a useful measured pattern, while avoiding a causal interpretation such as “age causes rankings to fall.”\n\n### Finding 2 — Click Capture by Position Tier\n\nFlyRank reports weighted CTR of about 0.420% for the top-3 tier versus about 0.050% for the Deep tier, describing a steep drop in click capture as ranking position worsens.\n\n**My methodology question:** The measured outcome is weighted CTR, calculated from clicks and impressions. Are the position-tier assignments and the CTR window defined independently enough to avoid circularity, and does the validation design support a claim about click capture beyond this observed cross-sectional association? In particular, could query mix, brand/non-brand traffic, device mix, or impression volume explain part of the difference between tiers?\n\nI would use this as directional evidence for prioritizing already-visible pages, not as proof that moving a page to a higher tier will itself cause the reported CTR increase.\n\n**Paper:** FlyRank, *The State of AI-Driven SEO — April 2026*. The paper states that headline findings rely mainly on direct aggregate comparisons and explicitly notes that the study finds patterns rather than proof of cause and effect."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Section 1 — record the paper findings and methodology questions as an auditable reference.\n",
    "paper_audit = {\n",
    "    'finding_1': 'Content Results Curve: health score varies by content age and is lower in older buckets after the peak.',\n",
    "    'question_1': 'How is the Health Score outcome constructed, and does the comparison separate age from site/content-mix differences?',\n",
    "    'finding_2': 'Click Capture by Position Tier: weighted CTR is much higher in top positions than in deep positions.',\n",
    "    'question_2': 'Are position tiers and CTR measured independently enough, and could query/device/brand mix explain part of the observed gap?'\n",
    "}\n",
    "for k, v in paper_audit.items():\n",
    "    print(f'{k}: {v}')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 2. My model under an honest split (before/after)\n\nThe Week-5 model predicts whether a page improves average position by at least 2 positions from March to April 2026. The decision-time features below are built only from March data.\n\n**Before:** random stratified train/test split.\n\n**After:** grouped split by `client_hash_id`, so the test clients are not represented in training. This directly tests whether the model carries over to clients it did not see.\n\nThe comparison uses the same features, target, model family, and metrics in both cases."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Section 2 — build the March-to-April modeling frame and compare random vs grouped validation.\n",
    "import duckdb\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "from google.colab import userdata\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, GroupShuffleSplit\n",
    "from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\n",
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, roc_auc_score\n",
    "\n",
    "con = duckdb.connect()\n",
    "HF_TOKEN = userdata.get('HF_TOKEN')\n",
    "con.execute(f\"CREATE OR REPLACE SECRET hf_secret (TYPE huggingface, TOKEN '{HF_TOKEN}')\")\n",
    "rel = 'hf://datasets/FlyRank/internship-warehouse'\n",
    "performance_path = f'{rel}/fact_content_daily_performance/**/*.parquet'\n",
    "\n",
    "query = f\"\"\"\n",
    "WITH march AS (\n",
    "    SELECT client_hash_id, content_hash_id,\n",
    "           SUM(gsc_impressions) AS march_impressions,\n",
    "           SUM(gsc_clicks) AS march_clicks,\n",
    "           AVG(gsc_avg_position) AS march_avg_position\n",
    "    FROM read_parquet('{performance_path}')\n",
    "    WHERE month = '2026-03' AND gsc_data_available IS TRUE\n",
    "    GROUP BY client_hash_id, content_hash_id\n",
    "),\n",
    "april AS (\n",
    "    SELECT client_hash_id, content_hash_id,\n",
    "           AVG(gsc_avg_position) AS april_avg_position\n",
    "    FROM read_parquet('{performance_path}')\n",
    "    WHERE month = '2026-04' AND gsc_data_available IS TRUE\n",
    "    GROUP BY client_hash_id, content_hash_id\n",
    ")\n",
    "SELECT m.*, a.april_avg_position,\n",
    "       CASE WHEN a.april_avg_position <= m.march_avg_position - 2 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS improved_2_positions\n",
    "FROM march m\n",
    "INNER JOIN april a USING (client_hash_id, content_hash_id)\n",
    "WHERE m.march_avg_position IS NOT NULL AND a.april_avg_position IS NOT NULL\n",
    "  AND m.march_impressions >= 1\n",
    "\"\"\"\n",
    "\n",
    "model_df = con.sql(query).df()\n",
    "model_df['march_ctr'] = model_df['march_clicks'] / model_df['march_impressions'].clip(lower=1)\n",
    "features = ['march_impressions', 'march_clicks', 'march_avg_position', 'march_ctr']\n",
    "X = model_df[features].replace([float('inf'), -float('inf')], pd.NA).dropna()\n",
    "y = model_df.loc[X.index, 'improved_2_positions'].astype(int)\n",
    "groups = model_df.loc[X.index, 'client_hash_id']\n",
    "\n",
    "print('Model rows:', len(X))\n",
    "print('Positive rate / base rate:', round(float(y.mean()), 4))\n",
    "print('Features:', features)\n",
    "\n",
    "model = Pipeline([\n",
    "    ('scale', StandardScaler()),\n",
    "    ('logreg', LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000, random_state=42))\n",
    "])\n",
    "\n",
    "# BEFORE: random stratified split\n",
    "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n",
    "    X, y, test_size=0.20, random_state=42, stratify=y\n",
    ")\n",
    "model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
    "pred = model.predict(X_test)\n",
    "prob = model.predict_proba(X_test)[:, 1]\n",
    "random_metrics = {\n",
    "    'split': 'Random stratified',\n",
    "    'accuracy': accuracy_score(y_test, pred),\n",
    "    'roc_auc': roc_auc_score(y_test, prob),\n",
    "    'test_positive_rate': y_test.mean(),\n",
    "    'base_rate': y.mean()\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "# AFTER: grouped split by client\n",
    "gss = GroupShuffleSplit(n_splits=1, test_size=0.20, random_state=42)\n",
    "train_idx, test_idx = next(gss.split(X, y, groups=groups))\n",
    "Xg_train, Xg_test = X.iloc[train_idx], X.iloc[test_idx]\n",
    "yg_train, yg_test = y.iloc[train_idx], y.iloc[test_idx]\n",
    "model.fit(Xg_train, yg_train)\n",
    "pred_g = model.predict(Xg_test)\n",
    "prob_g = model.predict_proba(Xg_test)[:, 1]\n",
    "grouped_metrics = {\n",
    "    'split': 'Grouped by client',\n",
    "    'accuracy': accuracy_score(yg_test, pred_g),\n",
    "    'roc_auc': roc_auc_score(yg_test, prob_g),\n",
    "    'test_positive_rate': yg_test.mean(),\n",
    "    'base_rate': y.mean()\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "comparison = pd.DataFrame([random_metrics, grouped_metrics])\n",
    "display(comparison.round(4))\n",
    "print('Interpret the gap, not just the score: a lower grouped score indicates that random-split performance may have benefited from repeated client-specific patterns.')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "### Validation interpretation\n\nThe grouped result is the more honest deployment-style estimate for this task because the model is evaluated on clients absent from training. If it is lower than the random result, that gap is itself evidence about how much the random split may have benefited from repeated client structure. Neither score should be presented as proof that a refresh causes improvement."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3. Leakage audit\n\nThe final feature set is deliberately limited to March decision-time signals. The April position is used only to construct the target and is never supplied to the model. This addresses the main leakage risks: label-derived features and future-window overlap.\n\nThe audit also checks whether any product decision flag or existing-system score is being used as an input. None is included in the feature list below."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Section 3 — explicit leakage audit\n",
    "leakage_audit = pd.DataFrame([\n",
    "    ['march_impressions', 'Low', 'March history; known before April target window.', 'Keep'],\n",
    "    ['march_clicks', 'Low', 'March history; known before April target window.', 'Keep'],\n",
    "    ['march_avg_position', 'Low', 'March average position; earlier than the April outcome.', 'Keep'],\n",
    "    ['march_ctr', 'Low', 'Derived only from March clicks/impressions.', 'Keep'],\n",
    "    ['april_avg_position', 'High if used as a feature', 'It is part of the future outcome used to create the label.', 'Target only; exclude from X'],\n    "    ['product flags / existing scores', 'High if used', 'Would encode an existing decision rather than independent evidence.', 'Not used']\n",
    "], columns=['item', 'risk', 'reason', 'decision'])\n",
    "display(leakage_audit)\n",
    "\n",
    "future_columns_in_features = [c for c in features if c.startswith('april_')]\n",
    "assert future_columns_in_features == [], f'Future columns leaked into features: {future_columns_in_features}'\n",
    "\n",
    "print('Leakage check passed: no April outcome column is present in the model feature list.')\n",
    "print('Timeline: March features -> April outcome.')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3B. Real failure examples\n\nThe following examples are model errors from the grouped test set. They are not cherry-picked client names or URLs; only anonymous IDs and measured fields are shown."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Failure examples from the grouped test set\n",
    "error_df = model_df.loc[Xg_test.index, ['client_hash_id', 'content_hash_id', 'march_avg_position', 'april_avg_position', 'improved_2_positions']].copy()\n",
    "error_df['predicted_probability'] = prob_g\n",
    "error_df['predicted_class'] = pred_g\n",
    "error_df['error'] = error_df['predicted_class'] != error_df['improved_2_positions']\n",
    "\n",
    "false_positive = error_df[(error_df['predicted_class'] == 1) & (error_df['improved_2_positions'] == 0)].sort_values('predicted_probability', ascending=False).head(5)\n",
    "false_negative = error_df[(error_df['predicted_class'] == 0) & (error_df['improved_2_positions'] == 1)].sort_values('predicted_probability', ascending=True).head(5)\n",
    "\n",
    "print('False positives: predicted improvement, but the measured target was not achieved.')\n",
    "display(false_positive[['client_hash_id', 'content_hash_id', 'march_avg_position', 'april_avg_position', 'predicted_probability']])\n",
    "print('False negatives: predicted no improvement, but the measured target was achieved.')\n",
    "display(false_negative[['client_hash_id', 'content_hash_id', 'march_avg_position', 'april_avg_position', 'predicted_probability']])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 4. Claim rewrite\n\n### Earlier/bolder claim\n\n> “The model can identify which pages will improve rankings after a content refresh.”\n\n### Evidence-safe rewrite\n\n> **Observed and measured:** the Week-5 Logistic Regression model provides **directional decision-support** for prioritizing pages based on March search-performance signals for a March-to-April position-improvement target. The grouped-by-client validation is the more honest test of generalization to unseen clients. The model does **not** establish that a refresh causes improvement, and it should not be treated as a guarantee of future ranking movement.\n\nThe wording deliberately uses measured/observed/directional/decision-support language and keeps causal claims out of scope."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Section 4 — compact claim record for the audit trail\n",
    "claim_record = {\n",
    "    'old_claim': 'The model can identify which pages will improve rankings after a content refresh.',\n",
    "    'safe_claim': 'The model provides directional decision-support for prioritizing pages for review against a measured March-to-April position-improvement target.',\n",
    "    'causal_claim_supported': False,\n",
    "    'guarantee_supported': False,\n",
    "    'validation_preferred': 'Grouped by client'\n",
    "}\n",
    "for k, v in claim_record.items():\n",
    "    print(f'{k}: {v}')"
   ]
  },
